One popular vulnerability that can be exploited to steal sensitive information from users is the Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability. XSS vulnerabilities occur when a web application accepts user input and includes it in the web page without proper sanitization or validation. This can lead to the execution of malicious scripts in the browser of a user who visits the vulnerable web page, allowing an attacker to steal sensitive information such as cookies, session tokens, or even passwords.


For example, suppose a popular social media platform has an XSS vulnerability in its comment section. An attacker could post a comment containing a malicious JavaScript snippet that, when executed, sends the user's session cookie to the attacker's server. Once the attacker has the session cookie, they can impersonate the user, gaining access to their account, viewing private messages, or even performing actions on behalf of the user, depending on the permissions granted to the user's account.


To exploit this vulnerability, an attacker would craft a comment with the malicious script, ensuring it gets executed in the context of a user's session. The script could look something like this:


